---
import ToolLayout from "@site/layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { referencesFor } from "@site/lib/references.ts";
import references from "../references.json";
import uk from "../data/targets/uk.json";
import us from "../data/targets/us.json";
import eu from "../data/targets/eu.json";
import meta from "../data/meta.json";
const { cite } = referencesFor(references as Parameters<typeof referencesFor>[0]);
const standards = [uk, us, eu];
---
<ToolLayout slug="nutrient-planner" page="methods">
  <h1>Methods and sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">How Food Finder and the Planner work, where their numbers come from, and what they can't tell you.</p>

  <h2>In brief</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Food values</strong> come from national food composition tables, each offered on its own and on an equal footing: the US (USDA SR Legacy) <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />, the UK (CoFID) <Fragment set:html={cite("cofid-2021")} />, Germany (BLS 4.0) <Fragment set:html={cite("bls-4")} />, France (Ciqual 2025) <Fragment set:html={cite("ciqual-2025")} />, Denmark (Frida 5.5) <Fragment set:html={cite("frida-55")} />, Finland (Fineli) <Fragment set:html={cite("fineli-20")} />, South Korea (KFCDb 9.3) <Fragment set:html={cite("kfcdb-93")} /> and Japan (MEXT, 8th edition) <Fragment set:html={cite("mext-2023")} />. Where a table says a value is estimated, calculated from a recipe, borrowed from another food or table, a trace or below a limit, that is kept with the value. Japanese food names are translated by this site and marked as such. Each value is the table's own, as published; a nutrient a table doesn't give is shown as not available, never as zero, and where a table defines a nutrient differently from the targets (for example vitamin A or energy factors), the page says so. Food Finder lists every food of every table; the Planner lists each table's basic foods (no dishes, sweets, fried or coated foods, offal or raw meat), because it suggests foods. As a beta option, you can choose "USDA + UK CoFID, blended": for foods found to be the same food in the UK table <Fragment set:html={cite("cofid-2021")} />, each value is the median of the two tables, with the range, each table's value and any caveats shown. The matching and blending are automated, without manual human verification. </li>
    <li><strong>Targets</strong> come from the official UK, US or EU recommended intakes for your age and sex.</li>
    <li><strong>Food Finder</strong> shows one food, in the amount you choose, against the same targets, charts and notes as the Planner. Targets set as a share of energy (fat, saturates, carbohydrate) are worked out for the middle of your daily calorie range, because one food is only part of a day.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions</strong> (Planner) are found by an optimiser running in your browser that gets as close as it can to every target without going over an upper limit.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing data stays missing</strong>: it is shown as NA (no data), never counted as zero.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small muted">Open a section below for the details. The limitations are listed at the end.</p>

  <details>
    <summary>Food composition data <span class="summary-note">where the food values come from</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <details>
        <summary>Which foods are included</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Nutrient values come from the US Department of Agriculture's FoodData Central (public domain), SR Legacy
          dataset <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />. About 115 cheap, common, UK-available foods are hand-matched to USDA records, given
          friendly names and practical units, and switched on by default. Around 2,100 more vegetables, fruits, pulses,
          grains, breads, nuts, seeds, meat, fish, dairy foods, eggs, oils, herbs and spices are added automatically by
          rules that keep cooked lean cuts of meat and leave out baby foods, branded and heavily processed products;
          these are off by default and can be switched on in the planner's food list, one by one, all at once, or as a "wider selection" of about 400 ordinary foods (a list chosen by judgement, not taken from a source; it is in the repository). US products with added nutrients (enriched or fortified) are marked "US-fortified" and aren't included when you switch on everything, because the UK versions may differ. Choosing vegetarian hides meat and
          fish; choosing vegan also hides dairy, eggs and honey. The food list, the rules and a match report live in the repository's
          <code>tools/nutrient-planner/</code> folder so anyone can check them.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>The one adjusted value: carbohydrate</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          One value is adjusted. USDA lists "Carbohydrate, by difference": what is left of a
          food after water, protein, fat, ash and alcohol, which includes fibre <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr-doc")} />. Dietary targets and food labels count
          carbohydrate without fibre, so the planner subtracts each food's fibre from it. A food with no fibre value
          (mostly meat, fish and oils) keeps USDA's figure.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Other national food tables <span class="summary-note">in preparation</span></summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>
            Food composition differs from country to country, and no single table covers every nutrient. Tables
            published by national food and health agencies (for example in the UK, France, Germany, the Nordic
            countries, Japan and Korea) are being checked so that a later version of the planner can offer them as
            alternatives. Each will be cited and archived like the USDA data, and each value will say how its source
            describes it (measured, calculated from a recipe, estimated, or borrowed from a similar food). Until then,
            only the USDA data is used.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <p class="muted small">
        Current snapshot: {meta.foodsLastUpdated ? `built ${meta.foodsLastUpdated}, ${meta.foodCount} foods` : "not built yet"}.
        The snapshot is stored in the repository so the site keeps working even if the USDA servers change, and is refreshed every three months.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Recommended intakes <span class="summary-note">UK, US and EU targets, and how they were checked</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      {standards.map((s) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{s.longName}</summary>
          <div class="details-body">
            <p class="small">
              {s.verified?.checked
                ? <><strong>Checked</strong> line by line against the original document ({s.verified.against}) on {s.verified.date}.</>
                : <><strong>Not yet checked</strong> against the original document. {s.verified?.against}.</>}
              {s.verified?.pending && <> <strong>Not yet checked:</strong> {s.verified.pending}</>}
            </p>
            <ul class="prose">
              {s.sources.map((src) => <li>{src.role ? `${src.role}: ` : ""}{src.title} <Fragment set:html={cite(src.id)} /></li>)}
            </ul>
            <ul class="prose muted small">{s.notes.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
      <p>
        The charts can also show the other two standards' targets for the same person (same age, sex, weight and
        calories) as thin coloured outlines, so you can see where the UK, US and EU agree and where they differ.
        The planner always optimises against the one standard you choose.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Food Finder <span class="summary-note">one food at a time</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        Food Finder is built from the Planner's own parts: the same food list, targets, charts, notes and references, so a
        change to the Planner shows there too. The differences are that you pick one food, and the details about you
        start from the Planner's defaults (female, 35, UK recommendations) until you open "About you" and change them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every food of every food table is listed, with its table in brackets, for example "(USDA)" or "(CoFID)"; the
        foods the beta blend covers also have a "blended, beta" entry
        (the median of the USDA and UK CoFID values, with the range shown). Amounts start at one unit (an egg, a slice,
        a banana, weighed as USDA publishes the portion <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />) or 100 g, as in the
        Planner's "Build my own", and can be changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every row is a share of a day's target. Targets that the source sets as a share of energy (fat, saturated fat and
        carbohydrate; EU thiamin and niacin) are worked out for the middle of your daily calorie range, as the targets
        table shows them, not for the food's own calories: measured against its own calories, almost any fatty food
        would read as "over the limit". The Planner measures each suggested day against that day's own calories, which
        its suggestions keep inside your range. A day you build yourself is measured the same way once it reaches your
        calorie range; until then, while it is only part of a day, it uses the middle of the range, as Food Finder does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How the planner chooses foods <span class="summary-note">the optimiser, variety between options, time limits</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <details>
        <summary>The optimiser</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          The planner solves a mixed-integer programme with the HiGHS solver <Fragment set:html={cite("highs")} />, running in your browser.
          It chooses at most your chosen number of ingredients and an amount of each, in practical steps
          (10 g, half an egg, a slice of bread; an egg, a slice or a banana weighs what USDA publishes for that food's portion <Fragment set:html={cite("usda-sr")} />: 50 g, 32 g and 118 g), to minimise the total <em>relative</em> shortfall across all
          minimum targets, so being 20% short of iron counts the same as being 20% short of protein. Upper limits
          (salt, saturated fat, tolerable upper intake levels) and your calorie range are never exceeded.
          Targets that the source sets as a share of energy (fat, saturated fat and carbohydrate as a percentage of energy; EU thiamin and niacin per unit of energy) are measured against each plan's own calories, not a fixed calorie figure.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Variety between options</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Further options are made to differ: each one shares fewer than half of its foods with any earlier option,
          and no food is used in more than half of the options (at least two). Where that would leave an option
          meeting the targets noticeably less well than the first option (by more than 2% of one target), a food may
          repeat more often, and if that still isn't enough, an option only has to change a third of each earlier
          option's foods. Such an option says so.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Preferences and time limits</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Among combinations that meet the targets equally well, the planner prefers the one with less food, so
          suggestions tend to sit near the bottom of your calorie range. It also has a mild preference for familiar
          foods: each food from outside the common list counts slightly against a combination, by no more in total
          than 1% of one target, so common foods are chosen unless another food does a noticeably better job.
          If a search over a long food list runs out of time, the common foods are searched as well and the better
          result is shown.
          Each search is limited to 5 seconds. If that limit is reached, the best combination found so far is shown and
          marked as such.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Vitamin D and oily fish</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Two things are deliberately not left to the optimiser. Vitamin D: few foods contain much, so suggestions don't try
          to reach the vitamin D target unless you tick the box for it; the result still shows how much the day provides and
          the NHS advice on supplements <Fragment set:html={cite("nhs-vitd")} />. Oily fish: suggestions include at most one
          portion (140 g) a day in total, and the NHS weekly limits are shown alongside <Fragment set:html={cite("nhs-fish")} />.
        </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Reading the charts <span class="summary-note">scales, colours and symbols</span></summary>
    <div class="details-body">
      <p>
        Each radar chart shows every nutrient as a percentage of its target. The thick navy ring is the target
        (100%). The scale is linear and runs to 150% unless a value is higher, in which case it grows to fit, so
        400% always looks larger than 300%; "Zoom to target" shows the area around 100% instead and marks anything
        beyond the edge with an arrow. Green points meet their goal and red ones don't. "≥" means aim for at least
        the target; "≤" means stay at or below it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The "At a glance" chart follows the nutrition declaration on UK and EU food labels (Regulation 1169/2011) <Fragment set:html={cite("eu-1169")} />:
        energy, fat, saturates, carbohydrate, sugars, fibre, protein and salt. Each is compared with the official
        recommendation for the person where one exists; total sugars, which has no recommendation, is compared with
        the label's adult reference intake (90 g) and shown in grey. Vitamins, minerals and essential amino acids
        (the building blocks of protein) each have their own chart below it, with a table showing which foods
        provide each nutrient.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <p>The results are estimates. Please treat them as a starting point for thinking about your food, not as a precise measurement.</p>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li><strong>Borrowed safety limits.</strong> The UK and EU tables used here don't give a complete set of upper limits, so the US tolerable upper intake levels <Fragment set:html={cite("nasem-dri")} /> are used for safety checks in every standard.</li>
    <li><strong>US food data, UK shops.</strong> Fortification differs between countries (for example US milk usually has added vitamin D; UK milk doesn't), and brands differ. Fortified products should be checked against their labels.</li>
    <li><strong>Iodine and some other nutrients</strong> are missing from most USDA records, so they are listed as "not checked" rather than guessed. Where a food has no value for a nutrient, it is not counted as zero: the amount is shown as "NA" (no data) if no food in the day has a value, or as "at least" the known total if some do, and the result is marked "unclear" rather than a pass when the missing data could change it. Suggestions are planned from known values only. EPA + DHA and niacin equivalents are only calculated for a food when every part is known.</li>
    <li><strong>Absorption isn't modelled yet.</strong> Iron from plants is absorbed less well than iron from meat, ALA omega-3 converts poorly to EPA and DHA, and phytates reduce zinc absorption. These are planned refinements.</li>
    <li><strong>Amino acid targets</strong> use the WHO safe intake per kg of body weight for adults (the average requirement plus 24%) <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2007")} />, and are applied to adults only. WHO found too little evidence to set separate values for older people.</li>
    <li><strong>Children.</strong> Targets for children come from the same official tables, but the suggested amounts are adult portion limits scaled down by calories, which is a rule of thumb. The planner is not designed for children under 5.</li>
    <li><strong>Reference values are for healthy populations.</strong> If you have a medical condition, are pregnant, or are planning food for a child, check with a doctor, midwife or registered dietitian.</li>
  </ul>

</ToolLayout>
